among logicians, the first <a class="xref" href="vol3.html#article-MODE">mode</a> of the first <a class="xref" href="vol2.html#article-FIGURE">figure</a> of syllogisms.

A syllogism in barbara, is one whereof all the <a class="xref" href="../1778/vol0.html#article-PROPOSITIONS">propositions</a> are <a class="xref" href="vol3.html#article-UNIVERSAL">universal</a> and affirmative; the middle <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-TERM">term</a> being the subject of the first proposition, and <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-ATTRIBUTE">attribute</a> in the <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-SECOND">second</a>. For example,

<a class="xref" href="vol1.html#article-BAR">Bar</a>. Every wicked <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-MAN">man</a> is miserable;
Ba. All tyrants are wicked men;
Ra. Therefore all tyrants are miserable.

